 as a carefully framed avowal, not as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg svarer: »Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Men da jeg siger Maria dette, græder hun, fordi hun forstaar, jeg\\ntaler sandt, og fordi hun er lykkelig.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.8,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression weakly functions as direct reassurance in response to Maria's fear that his change is temporary or self-deceptive. This contextual confirming function is secondary to T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"For det er jo sandt, ikke noget, Du imorgen fortryder, ikke\\nnoget, Du nu bilder Dig ind, fordi Du har længtes en Smule efter din\\nElskerinde?«\\n\\nJeg svarer: »Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At their reunion, Maria asks whether the narrator's apparent change is genuine rather than something he will regret or a temporary effect of longing. He answers her directly with the marked avowal, framed as a solemn assurance.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal words themselves contain no distinct undertaking. In the immediately following, separate assertions, the narrator presents his love as extending through pleasure, hardship, ordinary days, and celebrations, and says he wants to live a long time with Maria before dying with her. Maria's tears and happiness represent acceptance of the avowal as truthful, but the evidence does not show a separate explicit verbal acceptance of contractual terms.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognise the work with sufficient confidence from the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence is literary narration and strongly represents the focal avowal as sincere, but it cannot independently verify the speaker's inner state. The marked phrase is only a present-tense avowal; the more future-oriented language nearby is context and has not been treated as part of the target itself.\"}"
